                <h2>This Week's Observation: Account Management Approaches Are Diverging</h2>
<p>This week, several overseas companies reported a core contradiction in agency account services: when account numbers expanded from 3-5 to 15-20 or more, the original "group chat + spreadsheet + memory" model began to fail. </p>
<p>Specific manifestations include: operations staff needed to constantly switch between WeChat, email, and Excel sheets to confirm an account's opening progress, balance status, or recharge records; financial reconciliation often showed discrepancies between manually aggregated data and actual platform consumption; suspended accounts due to insufficient balance were often discovered by the media buyer themselves rather than by system alerts.</p>
<p>The root cause is not about human capability but a mismatch between tools and business scale. When account volume is small, manual management can suffice; but when operations reach a certain scale, systematic Kanban boards shift from "optional" to "essential."</p>
<h2>Why Agency Account Services Need Kanban Thinking</h2>
<p>Agency account services involve coordination across multiple stages: client requirement alignment, qualification review, platform submission, account activation, recharge processing, consumption monitoring, and reconciliation. In traditional models, information about these stages scatters across different communication records, lacking a unified display window.</p>
<p>Systematic Kanban boards transform this linear process into visual status cards. Yiyu's account management board adopts a typical Kanban structure:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Status Column</th>
<th>Content Included</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Pending Submission</td>
<td>Client qualification documents, account opening requirements</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Under Review</td>
<td>Platform review progress</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Activated</td>
<td>Account credentials, initial balance</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Recharge Pending Confirmation</td>
<td>Payment proof, account verification</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Active Campaign</td>
<td>Consumption curves, balance alerts</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Pending Reconciliation</td>
<td>Current period consumption details</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Each card represents key information for an account: platform, account ID, current balance, responsible person, next action item. When operations staff open the board, they can assess overall status within 3 seconds instead of spending 10 minutes scrolling through chat history.</p>
<p>The direct value of this visualization is twofold: reducing communication costs for information queries, and making abnormal status easier to detect—such as an account stuck in "Recharge Pending Confirmation" for over 48 hours, or an account balance approaching alert threshold without triggering notifications.</p>
<h2>This Week's Case: A Utility App Team's Account Management Upgrade</h2>
<p>This week, a utility app team served by Yiyu shared their management evolution path.</p>
<p>During their early overseas stages, this team managed 5 Meta ad accounts using simple online spreadsheets with columns including account name, balance, and operator. Since spreadsheet updates relied on manual entry, "update delays" frequently occurred—when staff completed payments, they often forgot to update the spreadsheet, causing data discrepancies during financial reconciliation.</p>
<p>After upgrading to systematic Kanban boards, they achieved automatic synchronization between recharge records and platform data. When recharge arrived, the system automatically updated card balances, eliminating the need for financial staff to manually verify each account. Meanwhile, the board set automatic alerts when balances fell below thresholds, notifying responsible parties immediately rather than discovering suspended accounts after the fact.</p>
<p>The team reported that after the management board went live, account-related information query time dropped from 40 minutes per person daily to under 10 minutes, with reconciliation error rates declining from approximately 5% to near zero.</p>
<h2>This Week's Recommendations: Three Actions for Immediate Implementation</h2>
<p>For overseas teams experiencing similar pain points, Yiyu recommends starting with these three actions:</p>
<p><strong>1. Audit Current Account Information Management Entry Points</strong></p>
<p>Identify where account information currently disperses (WeChat groups, emails, spreadsheets, local documents) and evaluate bottlenecks in information flow. This step requires no system transformation—only a thorough status quo review.</p>
<p><strong>2. Establish a Minimal Status Tracking Mechanism</strong></p>
<p>No need for a complete Kanban system from day one. Start with shared spreadsheets featuring simple status columns: account name, current status (opening/normal/suspended), balance, last update time, responsible operator. As long as everyone updates the same spreadsheet, information centralization improves significantly.</p>
<p><strong>3. Set Alert Rules for Key Nodes</strong></p>
<p>Establish alert thresholds based on business volume: who to notify when account balance falls below a certain amount, when to trigger follow-ups if account review exceeds certain days. These rules require no complex systems—a simple automation tool can implement them.</p>
<p>Tool selection doesn't need to be perfect from the start. The key is transitioning information from scattered to centralized, making status from vague to transparent. This week's change: increasingly, overseas teams recognize that manual management ceilings are constraining business scale, and systematic Kanban boards represent the direct path to breaking through those limits.</p>
